# geopress — geometric pressure as the morphology bound
# r_i = E_i^(1/3); contact repulsion (k/2)delta^2; energy flows
# down mu = (1/3)E^(-2/3) P, conserved by paired transfers.
# NO maximum size anywhere in the model.

== G1. BOUNDED SIZE vs COORDINATION (the test consonance failed) ==
Packing fraction is swept by box size, which sweeps the mean
contact number z. Consonance gave 5-13% locked at EVERY degree
with no trend; a working bound must instead stay sharp as z rises.
    L      z    r_mean    r_sd/r_mean    r_max/r_mean   dE/E      max|f|
  22.0   3.31    0.9631        0.0448          1.0759  +2.1e-16   8.0e-04
  20.0   3.66    0.9619        0.0573          1.1054  -2.1e-16   1.4e-03
  18.0   3.76    0.9606        0.0683          1.1577  -4.2e-16   1.5e-03
  16.0   3.89    0.9589        0.0811          1.1767  +0.0e+00   1.6e-03
  14.0   4.47    0.9565        0.0957          1.1997  -6.3e-16   5.5e-03
  12.0   7.33    0.9493        0.1318          1.2424  +0.0e+00   4.1e-02

reading: r_sd/r_mean is the width of the size distribution. If it
stays small and does not blow up as z rises, pressure bounds size
at every coordination — which is exactly what consonance could not
do. dE/E must sit at the FP floor: the transport is paired.

== G2. DOES A LOCALISED EXCESS HOLD? (the particle question) ==
Seed a central sphere with excess energy, then relax. A structure
that GROWS TO A SIZE AND STOPS — held by the surrounding pressure,
with no bound in the potential — is the object the program wants.
  boost   r_core/r_bulk t=0   after relax   retained   E_core frac
    2.0            1.2565        1.0952      0.371        0.0666
    4.0            1.5830        1.0695      0.119        0.0903
    8.0            1.9945        0.7536     -0.248        0.0880
   16.0            2.5129        0.4156     -0.386        0.0420
   32.0            3.1661        0.6137     -0.178        0.0989

reading: 'retained' near 0 = the excess dissolved (no particle);
near 1 = it held its contrast; >1 = runaway. A retained value that
SATURATES as the boost grows is the bound doing its job: more
energy stops buying proportionally more size.

== G3. NEGATIVE CONTROL — contact stiffness off ==
With k=0 there is no pressure, hence no mu, hence nothing to bound
or localise. Any structure surviving here is not the pressure's.
      k    r_sd/r_mean   r_max/r_mean   retained(boost 8)
   0.00        0.1953         1.9831               1.000
   0.01        0.1959         1.9790               0.047
   0.10        0.2010         1.9885              -0.102
   1.00        0.2045         1.9965              -0.248

reading: k=0 must show no bound and full retention (nothing moves
at all, since mu is identically zero). The bound and any
localisation must appear only as k rises.

== G4 (N16). DOES cap SATURATION LET A CORE HOLD? ==
k_ij = k/(1+(E_i+E_j)/2cap): a loaded pair goes soft, which puts a
NEGATIVE term in mu. cap=1e12 is the unsaturated model and must
reproduce G2 (retention about -0.25 at boost 8). If saturation
opens a coexistence region, retention should RISE as cap falls.
      cap   retained(boost 8)   r_sd/r_mean   r_max/r_mean   dE/E
    1e+12             -0.248         0.2045          1.9965  +6.5e-16
      100             -0.248         0.2045          1.9968  +3.2e-16
       30             -0.248         0.2046          1.9991  +0.0e+00
       10             -0.249         0.2049          2.0019  +6.5e-16
        3             -0.251         0.2055          2.0115  +4.9e-16
        1             -0.256         0.2060          2.0198  +3.2e-16
      0.3              0.047         0.2055          2.0241  +0.0e+00
      0.1              0.192         0.2023          2.0167  +8.1e-16

== G5. IS THE HELD CORE REAL, OR JUST SLOW? ==
Low cap means SOFT contacts, hence slower relaxation, so retention
could be incomplete dispersal rather than a held state. If the core
is a true coexisting phase, retention is FLAT in relaxation time.
If it decays toward the unsaturated -0.25, it was kinetics.
     cap   cycles   retained   max|f|      max|dE per sweep|
     0.1      120      0.192    4.2e-02         4.2e-04
     0.1      240      0.194    2.3e-02         1.9e-04
     0.1      480     -0.110    8.4e-03         5.4e-05
     0.1      960     -0.262    2.3e-03         3.0e-05
     0.3      120      0.047    3.7e-02         2.9e-04
     0.3      240     -0.260    1.2e-02         1.2e-04
     0.3      480     -0.260    3.5e-03         3.7e-05
     0.3      960     -0.260    2.9e-04         2.2e-06
