   1e+12      960     -0.248    6.2e-14         2.1e-16

== G6 (N17). THE CONTACT EXPONENT — where the falling branch is ==
Analytically, mu falls with E exactly when delta/r > (alpha-1)/2.
Harmonic (alpha=2) needs delta/r > 0.5, an order of magnitude past
typical jamming overlap — which is why every run dispersed. The
criterion depends ONLY on alpha, never on k, which is also why
N16's cap softening could not work. alpha->1 is a YIELDING contact
(force saturates with overlap): a cell under pressure converts
instead of storing more elastic energy.
Retention is reported at TWO relaxation times; N16 proved a single
number here is worthless.
  alpha  (a-1)/2   ret@240   ret@960   max|f|@960   mean delta/r
   2.00    0.500     -0.248     -0.248      6.2e-14         0.0000
   1.70    0.350     -0.095     -0.095      0.0e+00         0.0000
   1.40    0.200     -0.249     -0.249      0.0e+00         0.0000
   1.20    0.100     -0.177     -0.177      0.0e+00         0.0000
   1.10    0.050     -0.100     -0.100      0.0e+00         0.0000
   1.02    0.010     -0.101     -0.101      0.0e+00         0.0000

reading: coexistence should switch on where mean delta/r rises
above (alpha-1)/2. A core that holds must hold at BOTH relaxation
times; ret@240 positive with ret@960 negative is N16 all over again.

reading: FLAT retention across a 8x range of relaxation time, with
max|dE per sweep| falling toward zero, is a genuine coexisting
phase. Retention that keeps sliding toward -0.25 is the soft-contact
kinetics of low cap and nothing more.

reading: retention rising above 0 as cap falls = the core HOLDS,
and a value that saturates rather than growing without limit is a
bounded object. Watch r_max/r_mean at the same time: if the bound
blows up while retention rises, saturation bought localisation by
destroying the bound, which is not a win. dE/E must stay at the
FP floor throughout — the softening is symmetric, so transfers
stay paired.
